Certainly!  Loops are a way to repeatedly execute a block of code until a certain condition is met.  For example, consider this loop to print the numbers from 1 to 10:

for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);
}

This loop starts by setting the counter "i" to 1.  It then checks if the counter "i" is less than or equal to 10.  If it is, it sets "i" to 10 and increments "i" by 1.  It does this until "i" is no longer less than or equal to 10, at which point it increments "i" again and checks if it’s no longer less than or equal to 10.  In the end, the loop increments "i" each time by 1 until it hits 10 (when it should stop) and then it increments "i" again, printing out each of the numbers between 1 and 10.

